    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8a442a4e2d383feba7c0cb146c2a6d2cd7d2da140bf2ad0ae237445e52f2a23a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source separates two logical situations that look superficially similar: its Chapter C criteria are stated as biconditionals with RH, whereas the Chapter D.3 attacks use one-way premises A that would imply RH but have themselves been disproved. Thus exact equivalence supplies an alternative target without by itself supplying a tractable attack, while a valid sufficient implication becomes unusable when its required antecedent is false.",
